+/* From -orderWindow:relativeTo: before it does anything: a tab that is ordered in while another tab
+ * of its group is on screen takes that tab's toplevel; the tab on screen that is ordered out hands
+ * its toplevel to the tab beside it. YES: done here. */
+- (BOOL)_sfTabOrderWindow:(NSWindowOrderingMode)place relativeTo:(NSInteger)other
+{
+    NSWindowTabGroup *group = objc_getAssociatedObject(self, &SFTabGroupKey);
+    NSArray *windows = [group windows];
+
+    if (sfTabSwitching || [windows count] < 2)
+        return NO;
+    NSWindow *selected = [group selectedWindow];
+
+    if (place != NSWindowOut) {
+        if (selected != self && [selected isVisible] && ![self isVisible]) {
+            [group setSelectedWindow:self];
+            return YES;
+        }
+        return NO;
+    }
+    if (selected == self && [self isVisible]) {
+        NSUInteger i = [windows indexOfObject:self];
+        NSWindow *beside = [windows objectAtIndex:i + 1 < [windows count] ? i + 1 : i - 1];
+
+        [group setSelectedWindow:beside];
+        return ![self isVisible];
+    }
+    return NO;
+}
+
+// From -close: the window leaves its group (after it has handed its toplevel over)
+- (void)_sfTabWillClose
+{
+    [objc_getAssociatedObject(self, &SFTabGroupKey) removeWindow:self];
+}
